  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The solution to the inequality $2<9y-7$ is $y${{field-1}}{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $7$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & <9y-7 \\\\ 2+7 & <9y-7+7 \\\\ 9 & <9y+0 \\\\ 9 & <9y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & <9y \\\\ \\frac{9}{9} & <\\frac{9y}{9} \\\\ 1 & <\\frac{\\cancel{9}y}{\\cancel{9}} \\\\ 1 & <y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny>1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>1.$",
